Chapter: PrimIdentity
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Tier 9: object identity. `address-of` has 65 call sites in the compiler and
 had no assertion anywhere.

 It is the only builtin that can ask "are these two names the same object",
 which is the question finding 10 turned on: records are pointers, all of
 them, and `__record-set` mutates rather than copying, so a binding taken
 before an update sees the update. Tier 4 pins that through the VALUE. This
 file pins it through the ADDRESS, which is the same claim said a second way
 -- and two independent ways of saying it is what makes a regression test hard
 to fool. A plug that started copying would have to fake both.

 NO ADDRESS IS EVER PRINTED. The arms have different layouts and always will,
 so an address is the one thing that cannot be compared between them. Every
 line below compares two addresses to each other, or an address to itself, and
 prints yes or no. That is also why this could not simply be folded into tier
 4: the cost rows there print numbers, and here a number would be a bug.

 Two things worth predicting before reading the output. Taking the address of
 the same object twice must give the same answer, or the notion is meaningless.
 And an address must SURVIVE A MUTATION -- `__record-set` returns the same
 object, so the address before and the address after are equal, and if they
 differ then something copied and finding 10 has regressed on that arm.

 The list rows are the sharper ones, because a list is the type where a plug is
 most tempted to copy: `list-push` may reallocate its backing store, and the
 question is whether the LIST is still the same object when it does. Bare metal
 answers yes -- the block is one object and push mutates it. So the address of
 a list must not move when it grows, even though its contents may.

Section: Types

  mutable Cell = record { v : Integer }

  Pair = record { a : Integer, b : Integer }

Section: Instrument

  show-yn : Boolean -> Text
  show-yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

Section: Shapes

  three : Integer -> List Integer
  three (n) = [n, n + 1, n + 2]

  no-ints : Integer -> List Integer
  no-ints (n) = []

 Reads the address out of a record FIELD, which the inliner cannot collapse
 into the caller's own `address-of` the way a one-line wrapper can. The first
 draft used `addr-of-cell (c) = address-of c` and the emitted zig came out as
 `cx_address_of(c) == cx_address_of(c)` -- a tautology that would have passed
 on any arm, including a broken one.

  Boxed = record { held : Cell }

  addr-of-held : Boxed -> Integer
  addr-of-held (b) = address-of (b.held)

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni "-- an address is stable, or the notion means nothing --"
   let c = Cell { v = 1 }
   in act
     print-line-uni ("same object, twice asked   : " & show-yn (address-of c == address-of c))
     print-line-uni ("same through a record field: " & show-yn (address-of c == addr-of-held (Boxed { held = c })))
   end
   print-line-uni "-- distinct objects, distinct addresses --"
   let p = Cell { v = 1 }
   in let q = Cell { v = 1 }
   in act
     print-line-uni ("equal contents, two objects: " & show-yn (address-of p == address-of q == False))
     print-line-uni ("  (yes = same value did not make them the same object)")
   end
   print-line-uni "-- finding 10 through the address instead of the value --"
   let r = Cell { v = 1 }
   in let alias = r
   in let before = address-of r
   in let r2 = __record-set r "v" 99
   in act
     print-line-uni ("alias has the same address : " & show-yn (address-of alias == before))
     print-line-uni ("record-set returned the SAME: " & show-yn (address-of r2 == before))
     print-line-uni ("the address survived it    : " & show-yn (address-of r == before))
     print-line-uni ("and the value did change   : " & show-yn (alias.v == 99))
     print-line-uni ("  (all yes = one object, mutated, not copied)")
   end
   print-line-uni "-- a second record type, so this is not one shape's accident --"
   let s = Pair { a = 1, b = 2 }
   in let salias = s
   in act
     print-line-uni ("alias of a Pair is the same: " & show-yn (address-of salias == address-of s))
   end
   print-line-uni "-- lists: does growing move the object? --"
   let l = three 5
   in let lalias = l
   in let lbefore = address-of l
   in act
     print-line-uni ("alias shares the address   : " & show-yn (address-of lalias == lbefore))
     print-line-uni ("set-at does not move it    : " & show-yn (address-of (list-set-at l 1 99) == lbefore))
   end
   let g = three 5
   in let gbefore = address-of g
   in let g2 = list-push g 42
   in act
     print-line-uni ("push returns the same list : " & show-yn (address-of g2 == gbefore))
     print-line-uni ("and the original agrees    : " & show-yn (address-of g == gbefore))
     print-line-uni ("  (yes = growing did not make a new object)")
   end
   print-line-uni "-- a copy is a different object, which is the control --"
   let k = three 5
   in let kcopy = k & (no-ints 0)
   in print-line-uni ("`&` produced a new object  : " & show-yn (address-of kcopy == address-of k == False))
  end
